body:
### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?

Pages that draw their text into a `canvas` print badly: the printed canvas is the screen-resolution bitmap, so text is blurry on paper and not selectable in a PDF. 

**Browsers support**: Chromium and Gecko each have a way around this, **both outside the spec**. WebKit doesn't support it yet. But before supporting on WebKit, the WebKit team would want first to determine the behavior instead of just copying what other browsers are doing.

What HTML says today, in [the canvas element](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/canvas.html#the-canvas-element):

> In non-interactive, static, visual media, if the canvas element has been previously associated with a rendering context (e.g. if the page was viewed in an interactive visual medium and is now being printed, or if some script that ran during the page layout process painted on the element), then the canvas element represents embedded content with the element's current bitmap and size.

and in [Implementation notes](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/canvas.html#implementation-notes), implementations "can, instead of updating this bitmap, merely remember the sequence of drawing operations that have been applied to it until such time as the bitmap's actual data is needed".

So printing is defined as the bitmap, and keeping the drawing operations is allowed only as an optimization.

**Chromium.** During printing, and in a task that ran from `beforeprint`, a 2D canvas keeps its list of drawing operations and replays it into the print output. Only if the list starts from a fully cleared canvas: a clearRect or an opaque drawImage covering the whole canvas, `reset()`, a resize, or a new canvas. A fillRect covering the whole canvas does not count. Otherwise, after `putImageData`, or with `image-rendering: pixelated`, the bitmap is printed. All or nothing: a partial redraw in `beforeprint` prints the old bitmap. No author opt-in. ([html_canvas_element.cc](https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromium/src/+/686e88c911652661aa361b886162fd034747089b/third_party/blink/renderer/core/html/canvas/html_canvas_element.cc#1311), [canvas_rendering_context_2d.cc](https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromium/src/+/686e88c911652661aa361b886162fd034747089b/third_party/blink/renderer/modules/canvas/canvas2d/canvas_rendering_context_2d.cc#521))

**Gecko.** `canvas.mozPrintCallback = (state) => { draw(state.context); state.done(); }`. At print time the page gets a 2D context that draws straight into the print output. Explicit opt-in, and the page redraws everything.

Testcase (print, then save as PDF):

```html
<canvas width="10" height="10" style="width:200px;height:200px"></canvas>
<script>
const canvas = document.querySelector("canvas");
const draw = (ctx) => {
  ctx.clearRect(0, 0, 10, 10);
  ctx.font = '10px serif';
  ctx.fillText("A", 0, 10);
};
draw(canvas.getContext("2d"));
addEventListener("beforeprint", () => draw(canvas.getContext("2d")));
canvas.mozPrintCallback = (state) => { draw(state.context); state.done(); };
</script>
```

Chromium and Gecko: a sharp, selectable "A". WebKit: a 10×10 pixel "A" scaled up.

Questions:

1. Should a printed canvas be allowed (or expected) to use the drawing operations instead of the bitmap?
2. Implicit (Chromium, keyed on `beforeprint`) or explicit opt-in (Gecko, a callback)? An explicit opt-in avoids guessing which operations belong to the print.
3. If implicit, which operations count: since `beforeprint`, or since the last full clear? What is printed after a partial redraw?
4. What about operations that only exist as pixels (`putImageData`, `drawImage` of another canvas or video), WebGL and `ImageBitmapRenderingContext`, and a canvas transferred to an `OffscreenCanvas`?
5. `beforeprint` timing is already open in #5096. Does this depend on it?
